---
import Recording from "@/components/Recording.astro"
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro"
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
import { docsMetadata } from "@/lib/docs-meta"

const metadata = docsMetadata("/docs/usage/ui-modes")
---
<DocsLayout metadata={metadata}>
    <section>
      <h1 class="mt-0 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-4">UI modes</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground mb-8">The same <code>gooze run ./...</code> behaves differently on your laptop and in CI. Gooze
        picks its UI from whether output is a TTY (interactive) or not (for example CI or
        redirected output), so you get a live view locally and plain logs in pipelines.</p>
      <div>
        <div>
          <h2>TTY (interactive)</h2>
          <p>When running in a terminal, Gooze uses an interactive TUI. This is the first run of the{" "}<code>calc</code> package from the Quick start:</p>
          <Recording name="calc-first-run-tui" title="Recorded gooze run TUI on the calc package" tui />
          <p>After a run, open every mutation with its diff to see exactly what a survivor changed.
            Here <code>gooze report view --all</code> moves to the first survivor and opens its diff:</p>
          <Recording name="calc-report-view-all-tui" title="Recorded gooze report view --all session opening a survivor's diff" tui />
          <div class="mt-4">
            <p class="text-muted-foreground mb-2">Common keys in the viewer (<code>gooze report view --all</code>):</p>
            <ul>
              <li><code>↑/↓</code> or <code>j/k</code> to move</li>
              <li><code>/</code> to filter</li>
              <li><code>enter</code> / <code>space</code> to toggle the diff</li>
              <li><code>q</code> to quit</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">More details: <a href="/docs/cli/report/#view"><code>gooze report view</code></a>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2>Non-TTY (simple/CI)</h2>
          <p>When output is redirected (or in CI), Gooze uses a simple UI.</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={"gooze run ./... | cat"} />
          <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">This example forces non-interactive mode by piping output.
            It is useful when you want stable CI logs.</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground"><code>gooze report view</code> without <code>--all</code> prints the same plain
            summary in both modes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
</DocsLayout>
